1
00:00:11,661 --> 00:00:23,634
[SPEAKER_01]: There are different reasons that people would be meditating for, but, eh... based on the suttas, meditation should be developed, should help develop understanding.

2
00:00:23,674 --> 00:00:27,078
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about, eh... switching things off.

3
00:00:28,912 --> 00:00:36,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And absorbing yourself in some states whereby nothing else will be... will be present in your awareness.

4
00:00:37,776 --> 00:00:47,981
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really about surmounting the states, surmounting the unwholesome states, surmounting the states that cause us to suffer, through understanding them.

5
00:00:50,242 --> 00:00:50,922
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

6
00:00:54,704 --> 00:00:57,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, really, practice of meditation should be a practice of...

7
00:00:58,741 --> 00:01:11,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Of developing your mind, of finding a place in your mind that then you can maintain throughout the day, even when you are not actively meditating.

8
00:01:13,711 --> 00:01:27,900
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, and that's why in other talks we would often say that guided meditation, guided contemplation, we would use it interchangeably, because it is, it is, it's pretty much

9
00:01:29,092 --> 00:01:34,096
[SPEAKER_01]: a right type of contemplation that then results in certain imperturbability of your mind.

10
00:01:34,717 --> 00:01:36,659
[SPEAKER_01]: And, in that way, it's a meditation.

11
00:01:38,841 --> 00:01:43,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, we can... like now, we can... we can...

12
00:01:45,725 --> 00:02:02,213
[SPEAKER_01]: You can contemplate, pick up a state that bothers you throughout the day, or causes you issues, whatever that is, and then you can reflect on it, and learn how to discern it rightly.

13
00:02:04,074 --> 00:02:08,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you can imagine, for example, the anger.

14
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:10,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say anger.

15
00:02:10,857 --> 00:02:11,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That can be our example here.

16
00:02:15,715 --> 00:02:17,056
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that can be your meditation.

17
00:02:18,317 --> 00:02:40,715
[SPEAKER_01]: You can set some quiet time aside, prevent any disturbances, half an hour, whatever you can do, and then actively think upon the present experience, without losing the sight of that present experience.

18
00:02:42,617 --> 00:02:47,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to know how to... you want to learn how to think without greed, aversion, delusion.

19
00:02:49,081 --> 00:03:02,109
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the phenomenon of anger... you don't have to be angry to... when you... when you... when you want to do this meditation, but just bring it to the fore.

20
00:03:02,589 --> 00:03:05,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember when you are angry.

21
00:03:05,891 --> 00:03:11,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're investigating the phenomenon of anger, the phenomenon of the whole world.

22
00:03:14,255 --> 00:03:22,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be trying to get rid of it or imagine a state whereby it will never arise.

23
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:26,162
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be... you want to understand it.

24
00:03:26,462 --> 00:03:35,628
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see to what extent that which makes you angry is inevitable and then to what extent there is your responsibility in regard to it.

25
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:43,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of anger, just imagine

26
00:03:44,306 --> 00:03:45,627
[SPEAKER_01]: the times when you were angry.

27
00:03:48,249 --> 00:03:50,731
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can let it endure, let it be present for a while.

28
00:03:52,593 --> 00:03:59,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't overthink it, but certainly don't... don't try to not think it either.

29
00:04:04,263 --> 00:04:07,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can ask yourself, like, for example, imagine when you were angry.

30
00:04:12,129 --> 00:04:12,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can imagine

31
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:26,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Many other times, if you're angry or irritated, if you're not getting angry, maybe irritated or annoyed, what is in common to all of those experiences of anger or irritation?

32
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:30,042
[SPEAKER_01]: What is a common thread that unites them all?

33
00:04:31,724 --> 00:04:32,544
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature?

34
00:04:36,107 --> 00:04:39,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can answer this question in many ways and

35
00:04:40,922 --> 00:04:46,166
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not crucial to always answer it in the same way, because really you're not... you're investigating.

36
00:04:47,548 --> 00:04:52,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes your investigation will lead you to different aspects of it, of phenomenon.

37
00:04:53,373 --> 00:04:59,558
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... just keep pressing with the questions, while the phenomenon is enduring.

38
00:05:00,579 --> 00:05:05,683
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the phenomenon of anger, all the times I was angry, irritated, annoyed.

39
00:05:07,164 --> 00:05:08,025
[SPEAKER_01]: What is in common?

40
00:05:11,176 --> 00:05:18,641
[SPEAKER_01]: See what you come up with.

41
00:05:31,149 --> 00:05:35,612
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the most obvious things that is in common is, eh... it's unpleasant.

42
00:05:37,547 --> 00:05:43,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Every single time you experience anger, it's unpleasant.

43
00:05:43,992 --> 00:05:45,714
[SPEAKER_01]: That experience is not nice.

44
00:05:48,516 --> 00:05:50,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you can ask a second question.

45
00:05:51,619 --> 00:06:01,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be angry without that little sting of displeasure?

46
00:06:02,508 --> 00:06:03,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any anger?

47
00:06:04,217 --> 00:06:08,460
[SPEAKER_01]: There is truly, truly enjoyable and pleasant experience of anger.

48
00:06:09,081 --> 00:06:18,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might have like a secondary experience of pleasure, like getting revenge or getting back at someone through anger, and then you get their satisfaction.

49
00:06:18,949 --> 00:06:20,290
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not what we are talking about.

50
00:06:20,570 --> 00:06:26,154
[SPEAKER_01]: We're talking about the actual, the very first, originally enduring phenomenon of anger.

51
00:06:27,355 --> 00:06:29,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that pleasant or unpleasant?

52
00:06:29,857 --> 00:06:30,858
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always unpleasant.

53
00:06:34,116 --> 00:06:45,842
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no single exception, because if you weren't unpleasant... Say something... Say like this... Somebody does something that upsets you, that irritates you.

54
00:06:45,962 --> 00:06:49,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... that's... that's your anger.

55
00:06:49,884 --> 00:06:56,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You're irritated, you got angry, because you were upset, for whatever reason.

56
00:06:56,807 --> 00:07:02,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, say, on some other occasion, you're in a good mood, and somebody does the same thing to you.

57
00:07:04,137 --> 00:07:09,440
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you really did not experience that sting of discomfort through that upset.

58
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:11,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not angry.

59
00:07:12,321 --> 00:07:13,302
[SPEAKER_01]: You might even laugh it off.

60
00:07:15,443 --> 00:07:21,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize that anger then, it's not determined, directly determined by what's done to you.

61
00:07:21,707 --> 00:07:23,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really determined whether by...

62
00:07:24,848 --> 00:07:31,112
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really determined by whether you're experiencing discomfort on account of what's done to you.

63
00:07:35,767 --> 00:07:44,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's really the discomfort, coming in contact, having discomfort arise in your experience.

64
00:07:45,154 --> 00:07:47,675
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where your anger is rooted in.

65
00:07:49,397 --> 00:07:53,339
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not rooted in circumstances that bring that anger forward.

66
00:07:55,801 --> 00:08:03,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you are angry, irritated, annoyed, means to some degree you are upset.

67
00:08:03,546 --> 00:08:04,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

68
00:08:05,396 --> 00:08:12,818
[SPEAKER_01]: How hard you find that upset might be, it's still the upset, which means there is a degree of discomfort, displeasure, unpleasant feeling.

69
00:08:14,579 --> 00:08:15,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Every single time.

70
00:08:16,399 --> 00:08:24,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And every single time you will get upset in the future, there will be the common thread.

71
00:08:24,681 --> 00:08:34,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, really, when you're getting angry over in the present, when you've been getting over... angry over in the past, or you will get angry over in the future,

72
00:08:35,223 --> 00:08:40,828
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, eh... it's you being liable to discomfort on the emotional level.

73
00:08:42,189 --> 00:08:49,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you experiencing unpleasant feelings is what makes you angry.

74
00:08:52,718 --> 00:08:53,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

75
00:08:55,427 --> 00:08:59,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try now, like, jump and look into an immediate solution.

76
00:08:59,709 --> 00:09:01,069
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I stop doing this?

77
00:09:01,729 --> 00:09:05,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not on the level of doing as you would be performing any other action.

78
00:09:05,631 --> 00:09:06,591
[SPEAKER_01]: It's subtler than that.

79
00:09:07,612 --> 00:09:10,272
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop doing it by understanding it.

80
00:09:11,073 --> 00:09:16,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop doing it by ceasing to act out of ignorance of it.

81
00:09:17,942 --> 00:09:19,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the ways of stop doing it.

82
00:09:19,242 --> 00:09:24,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you stop acting out of ignorance of your anger?

83
00:09:24,544 --> 00:09:27,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you stop ignoring the true nature of anger.

84
00:09:30,706 --> 00:09:32,867
[SPEAKER_01]: You learn how to maintain the mindfulness of it.

85
00:09:33,503 --> 00:09:34,704
[SPEAKER_01]: are to allow it to endure.

86
00:09:35,184 --> 00:09:35,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And you dwell on it.

87
00:09:35,924 --> 00:09:49,112
[SPEAKER_01]: You actively make the effort, as we said when we started this, to think it concretely, think it properly in the present moment, not ignore it, not justify it, not provide random satis... emotionally satisfying answers to it.

88
00:09:50,673 --> 00:10:01,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by dwelling on the nature of anger, clarifying it further and further and further, undoing all the layers of ignoring that you developed

89
00:10:03,555 --> 00:10:07,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Through blindly, eh... ignorantly acting out of anger all this time.

90
00:10:08,579 --> 00:10:14,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And mind you, all this, what we were describing, is the same principle towards sensuality, lust, towards anything unwholesome.

91
00:10:16,625 --> 00:10:18,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to restrain yourself first.

92
00:10:19,508 --> 00:10:21,629
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're angry, don't give in to anger.

93
00:10:23,791 --> 00:10:31,077
[SPEAKER_01]: When you haven't been giving in to anger, then you can start reflecting on the nature of anger, eh... correctly, like what we just... what we just done.

94
00:10:33,103 --> 00:10:39,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you're angry, every time there was an anger in your experience, it was rooted in discomfort.

95
00:10:42,129 --> 00:10:43,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can they be pressed further?

96
00:10:43,770 --> 00:10:44,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you investigate further?

97
00:10:47,553 --> 00:10:53,336
[SPEAKER_01]: What is in common to every single discomfort that you ever experienced?

98
00:10:56,078 --> 00:10:58,480
[SPEAKER_01]: What kind of attitude was in the background of it?

99
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:00,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Your attitude.

100
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:13,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time you encountered unpleasant feeling, or a prospect of an unpleasant feeling, what was the... what was the background of it?

101
00:11:13,665 --> 00:11:20,168
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the common thread through all these different phenomena of unpleasant experiences?

102
00:11:22,229 --> 00:11:22,789
In the past,

103
00:11:31,493 --> 00:11:46,766
[SPEAKER_01]: In the future, doesn't matter how many, how few, if any phenomena of unpleasant nature has arisen, it must have a taken in a certain nature of being unpleasant.

104
00:11:47,466 --> 00:11:48,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that nature?

105
00:11:49,848 --> 00:11:51,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, not theoretically.

106
00:11:54,252 --> 00:12:01,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, what is in common to every displeasure you ever went through and you ever will... and you will ever go through?

107
00:12:10,115 --> 00:12:11,956
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not wanted, isn't it?

108
00:12:16,397 --> 00:12:23,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time there's a genuinely, emotionally experienced discomfort, it's not wanted.

109
00:12:24,751 --> 00:12:25,571
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's in common.

110
00:12:28,992 --> 00:12:29,672
[SPEAKER_01]: You resist it.

111
00:12:31,253 --> 00:12:32,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it to not be there.

112
00:12:34,574 --> 00:12:38,135
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel entitled to wanting it to not be there.

113
00:12:43,416 --> 00:12:48,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you were angry, means

114
00:12:51,369 --> 00:12:56,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Every one... every... every of those time you are experiencing a form of discomfort.

115
00:12:57,873 --> 00:13:06,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time there's a form of discomfort, every time there's a form of unpleasant feeling, every time there is a resistance to it.

116
00:13:07,859 --> 00:13:17,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can safely assume that whenever there is an anger, experience of anger, there is resistance to it.

117
00:13:21,248 --> 00:13:22,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to get rid of it.

118
00:13:29,071 --> 00:13:29,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

119
00:13:37,215 --> 00:13:40,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Why you try get... Why is that and what are you trying to get rid of it?

120
00:13:43,937 --> 00:13:49,740
[SPEAKER_01]: What exactly should not be there from that point of view?

121
00:13:53,938 --> 00:14:01,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it circumstances that upset us, that caused us that discomfort, or the discomfort itself?

122
00:14:03,125 --> 00:14:05,527
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your craving aiming at, claiming to get rid of?

123
00:14:14,835 --> 00:14:15,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always a discomfort.

124
00:14:15,996 --> 00:14:18,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are aiming at the circumstances,

125
00:14:19,785 --> 00:14:25,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You do so, so to remove the basis for that displeasure that you are being touched by.

126
00:14:27,749 --> 00:14:37,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if, theoretically speaking now, you can imagine to not resist the displeasure, to stop resisting it.

127
00:14:38,954 --> 00:14:47,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not like battle the circumstances, defend, attack, prevent,

128
00:14:49,026 --> 00:14:59,891
[SPEAKER_01]: But just internally, whenever there is a discomfort, you're mindful of it, and make sure there is no giving in to the attitude of, I don't want this.

129
00:15:01,552 --> 00:15:05,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you want it, but means you stop not wanting it.

130
00:15:07,354 --> 00:15:08,775
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the subtlety of the Middle Way.

131
00:15:09,756 --> 00:15:12,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Opposite of wanting...

132
00:15:12,617 --> 00:15:14,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Opposite of not wanting, is not wanting.

133
00:15:15,506 --> 00:15:16,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, let's put it like this.

134
00:15:17,107 --> 00:15:24,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting is not the opposite of not wanting, phenomenologically speaking.

135
00:15:26,572 --> 00:15:29,133
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to be very accurate, which you must.

136
00:15:29,854 --> 00:15:34,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Opposite of not wanting is the absence of not wanting.

137
00:15:36,257 --> 00:15:38,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the presence of wanting.

138
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:45,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, when there is resistance,

139
00:15:48,505 --> 00:15:55,147
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean now you must learn how to want the unpleasant feeling in order to get rid of that resistance.

140
00:15:55,967 --> 00:15:58,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It only means you need to stop resisting it.

141
00:15:58,748 --> 00:16:03,489
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to overdo it and go the other way.

142
00:16:05,490 --> 00:16:13,432
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when the Buddha said he crossed the shore by not pushing forward, by not pulling backwards, by not standing still.

143
00:16:14,498 --> 00:16:15,959
[SPEAKER_01]: When they say, well, how did you do that?

144
00:16:16,699 --> 00:16:19,981
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, well, I didn't push forward by not pushing forward.

145
00:16:20,482 --> 00:16:22,803
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't pull backwards by not pulling backwards.

146
00:16:23,403 --> 00:16:26,505
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't say, I didn't pull backwards by pushing forward.

147
00:16:27,766 --> 00:16:31,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the absence of pulling backwards is how you stop pulling backwards.

148
00:16:31,908 --> 00:16:36,751
[SPEAKER_01]: The absence of resisting the unpleasant feeling is how you stop resisting it.

149
00:16:37,472 --> 00:16:41,954
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people say, eh... hate is only conquered by love, that's not true.

150
00:16:42,395 --> 00:16:42,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

151
00:16:44,201 --> 00:16:46,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Hate is conquered by non-hate.

152
00:16:46,863 --> 00:16:48,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Love is conquered by non-love.

153
00:16:53,447 --> 00:17:02,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't be swinging from one end to the other, or rather, you won't be able to help, but to, initially, but if you start discerning that

154
00:17:04,876 --> 00:17:12,338
[SPEAKER_01]: If you learn how to not resist anger, that doesn't mean you must love the circumstances that caused it, or anything like it.

155
00:17:12,838 --> 00:17:14,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to stop resisting it.

156
00:17:14,499 --> 00:17:19,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will stop resisting it, when you truly understand the nature of unpleasant feeling.

157
00:17:21,441 --> 00:17:24,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's get back to the practical side of it, as we've been discussing it.

158
00:17:25,602 --> 00:17:27,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember all the times you were angry.

159
00:17:27,722 --> 00:17:28,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, bring it back.

160
00:17:30,503 --> 00:17:31,243
[SPEAKER_01]: We drifted away.

161
00:17:31,303 --> 00:17:33,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring it back.

162
00:17:33,944 --> 00:17:34,164
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

163
00:17:35,977 --> 00:17:42,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Every single time you were irritated, annoyed, angry, upset, every one of those times, it was unpleasant.

164
00:17:43,143 --> 00:17:50,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being irritated, angry, upset, means being exposed to displeasure, enduring it, being touched by it.

165
00:17:52,570 --> 00:18:04,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, we already, to a degree, transcended, went beyond the particular circumstances that might have made you angry or upset.

166
00:18:06,379 --> 00:18:07,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Who said what?

167
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:07,981
[SPEAKER_01]: What happened to you?

168
00:18:08,241 --> 00:18:09,803
[SPEAKER_01]: All that, it's really secondary.

169
00:18:11,224 --> 00:18:15,929
[SPEAKER_01]: You are angry, not because there is displeasure present.

170
00:18:16,529 --> 00:18:19,933
[SPEAKER_01]: You're angry because the present displeasure is not wanted.

171
00:18:21,655 --> 00:18:27,661
[SPEAKER_01]: You take that for granted, then your actions, angry actions, will be coming out of it.

172
00:18:29,162 --> 00:18:29,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

173
00:18:31,052 --> 00:18:36,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is phenomenologically secondary to your resistance to displeasure.

174
00:18:36,433 --> 00:18:40,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is your reaction to your own resistance.

175
00:18:40,874 --> 00:18:45,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you resist the discomfort and that in itself, it's quite uncomfortable.

176
00:18:46,296 --> 00:18:55,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you add another layer of then trying to to get rid of your attempts of getting rid of the unpleasant feeling.

177
00:18:59,626 --> 00:19:08,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, what if you just stop resisting the unpleasant feeling?

178
00:19:13,433 --> 00:19:16,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to the degree you've been resisting it, you stop resisting it.

179
00:19:18,515 --> 00:19:24,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to the degree that there is the attitude of this is not wanted, to that degree you remove that attitude.

180
00:19:27,359 --> 00:19:28,580
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't do that

181
00:19:29,613 --> 00:19:33,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally, as in, you can't decide, make a decision, or stop resisting it.

182
00:19:34,857 --> 00:19:39,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it... that attitude precedes... your intentions are a result of that attitude.

183
00:19:39,301 --> 00:19:46,426
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would say he avoids acting upon intentions that are rooted in unwholesome, and he acts upon intentions that are rooted in wholesome.

184
00:19:46,486 --> 00:19:48,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Wholesome is absence of aversion.

185
00:19:51,230 --> 00:19:54,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will stop maintaining that attitude,

186
00:19:56,083 --> 00:20:00,065
[SPEAKER_01]: When you fully discern that in regard to which your attitude is maintained.

187
00:20:00,946 --> 00:20:03,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasant feeling.

188
00:20:03,607 --> 00:20:06,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we imagine times we were angry.

189
00:20:06,729 --> 00:20:09,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you may see what the common thread in that is.

190
00:20:09,731 --> 00:20:10,672
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the discomfort.

191
00:20:11,112 --> 00:20:13,513
[SPEAKER_01]: See what the common thread in every single discomfort is.

192
00:20:14,414 --> 00:20:17,816
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the unpleasant... it's the resisting of that unpleasant feeling.

193
00:20:18,952 --> 00:20:31,238
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is, again, this is not like something that will lead us to some sort of great conclu... well, it might lead you to some great conclusions, but it's more like the value of this is in being repetitively done.

194
00:20:32,198 --> 00:20:36,300
[SPEAKER_01]: This self-questioning and and the reflection, contemplation, meditation.

195
00:20:37,441 --> 00:20:42,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a woman, he keeps reviewing his mind for greed, aversion, delusion.

196
00:20:43,404 --> 00:20:46,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like you do it once, you provide the answers and that's it, you're done.

197
00:20:46,965 --> 00:20:47,105
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

198
00:20:47,565 --> 00:20:48,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you do it again.

199
00:20:49,452 --> 00:20:56,058
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, you do it when the context that you have developed through providing these answers ceases.

200
00:20:56,598 --> 00:20:59,781
[SPEAKER_01]: When you get distracted from it and so on, you redo it.

201
00:21:00,642 --> 00:21:04,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might arrive at different contexts, but the point is you keep doing it.

202
00:21:05,186 --> 00:21:11,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Because through doing it, you're removing your own layers of ignoring it, of being unfamiliar with it.

203
00:21:12,373 --> 00:21:14,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one of the main things that everyone... well,

204
00:21:16,533 --> 00:21:23,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what the undeveloped minds are unfamiliar with, is the nature of the unpleasant feeling.

205
00:21:23,815 --> 00:21:30,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we were looking at the nature of anger, that brought us to the, well, nature of discomfort, that brought us to the nature of resisting the discomfort.

206
00:21:32,196 --> 00:21:36,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can now... you could even say, what is the nature of resistance then?

207
00:21:37,057 --> 00:21:39,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of resistance, craving for things to not be.

208
00:21:40,836 --> 00:21:42,676
[SPEAKER_01]: What is in common to every single one of those?

209
00:21:42,716 --> 00:21:47,958
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in common that they're bound up with every single time you experienced unpleasant feeling.

210
00:21:50,858 --> 00:21:55,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that begs the question then, can you experience unpleasant feeling without trying to get rid of it?

211
00:21:56,519 --> 00:22:03,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much your first step towards practical application of this very contemplation.

212
00:22:03,221 --> 00:22:08,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're sitting quietly, meditating like this, contemplating, but now, when you go back to the world,

213
00:22:09,737 --> 00:22:17,882
[SPEAKER_01]: When unpleasantness touches you, and inevitably will, you remember to try what you've been discerning.

214
00:22:18,462 --> 00:22:20,704
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I stop resisting this?

215
00:22:20,744 --> 00:22:20,984
[SPEAKER_01]: This.

216
00:22:21,064 --> 00:22:24,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... not the circumstances.

217
00:22:24,446 --> 00:22:27,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm restrained in regards to circumstances, but they were never the problem.

218
00:22:28,848 --> 00:22:29,789
[SPEAKER_01]: He said this or that.

219
00:22:29,809 --> 00:22:36,713
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not... the problem is that the discomfort touches me and automatically upon being touched, I simultaneously don't want that.

220
00:22:37,394 --> 00:22:37,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

221
00:22:40,368 --> 00:22:47,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I maintain the view that I have a... that I'm in a position to prevent feelings from manifesting?

222
00:22:49,241 --> 00:22:51,805
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing that we arrived at now.

223
00:22:52,867 --> 00:22:56,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you... could you be resisting anything?

224
00:22:57,826 --> 00:22:59,487
[SPEAKER_01]: If that thing hasn't arisen beforehand.

225
00:23:00,187 --> 00:23:11,794
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say beforehand, I mean right there at the same time, but before your attitude, or rather, more fundamental to your attitude, the basis for your attitude.

226
00:23:12,674 --> 00:23:21,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your attitude of resisting displeasure is simultaneous to displeasure, but displeasure is first, in that structural, phenomenological manner.

227
00:23:23,740 --> 00:23:26,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, would you be able to be resisting

228
00:23:27,985 --> 00:23:33,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Any feeling, if that feeling is not there on its own, manifested, for you to resist it.

229
00:23:37,610 --> 00:23:56,981
[SPEAKER_01]: To bring into mind, any time you experience any form of discomfort, emotionally, like even now, just abstractly, you think about the word pain, and it already has a significance, implied, implicit significance of not wanted, to be prevented,

230
00:24:03,825 --> 00:24:05,045
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you stop that?

231
00:24:06,246 --> 00:24:07,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you not go and want pain?

232
00:24:08,467 --> 00:24:10,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you just stop not wanting of it?

233
00:24:12,308 --> 00:24:13,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be indifferent to it?

234
00:24:14,869 --> 00:24:16,150
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically what we are saying.

235
00:24:20,051 --> 00:24:23,693
[SPEAKER_01]: And the good news is, you can, if you understand the nature of it.

236
00:24:32,108 --> 00:24:34,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any say in its arising?

237
00:24:37,972 --> 00:24:43,835
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, why did it then arise in the first place, when it's not wanted from the start?

238
00:24:45,756 --> 00:24:46,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you didn't, really.

239
00:24:47,417 --> 00:24:49,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you did, it wouldn't have arisen.

240
00:24:54,322 --> 00:24:57,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unpleasant feelings then, arise on their own.

241
00:25:00,566 --> 00:25:01,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that a problem?

242
00:25:04,376 --> 00:25:16,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we gave the example when we started this, on many occasions, the same things that sometimes make you suffer arise, and then... but you don't feel unpleasant on account of them.

243
00:25:19,924 --> 00:25:26,988
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those things, they cause that, seemingly, cause that displeasure, are not always unpleasant.

244
00:25:28,909 --> 00:25:33,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, sometimes they might be, sometimes they might not be, but...

245
00:25:33,231 --> 00:25:33,451
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

246
00:25:35,041 --> 00:25:42,624
[SPEAKER_01]: They're only the problem when they're unpleasant.

247
00:25:45,165 --> 00:25:49,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means then, the problem is not the things that cause you displeasure.

248
00:25:51,428 --> 00:25:52,528
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is the displeasure.

249
00:25:54,069 --> 00:25:56,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And displeasure doesn't always arise.

250
00:25:58,951 --> 00:26:00,632
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it does, it's a problem.

251
00:26:01,032 --> 00:26:02,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of your attitude of trying to get rid of it.

252
00:26:16,533 --> 00:26:34,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, would you be able to maintain the attitude of trying to get rid of the displeasure, if you're not holding onto a view that you can get rid of that displeasure?

253
00:26:42,885 --> 00:26:57,013
[SPEAKER_01]: If you... if you truly, completely realize that the domain of displeasure or pleasure, domain of feeling, is inaccessible to you, would you still be acting as if you have access to it?

254
00:26:58,754 --> 00:27:01,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you even entertain such notions in your thoughts?

255
00:27:22,683 --> 00:27:33,452
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the feelings, on account of which you have attitude of wanting them or not wanting them and so on, you have no say in their arising.

256
00:27:34,212 --> 00:27:35,593
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say whatsoever.

257
00:27:39,817 --> 00:27:46,062
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you then maintain the attitude as if you do have a say?

258
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:57,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we just clarified, discovered, you wouldn't be trying to get rid of the unpleasant feeling, unless there is an implicit assumption that you can get rid of it.

259
00:28:00,142 --> 00:28:05,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you look at the unpleasant feeling, you realize it has a reason on its own, fully enclosed, independent of you.

260
00:28:07,144 --> 00:28:09,064
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no say in it.

261
00:28:09,244 --> 00:28:13,225
[SPEAKER_01]: All you can do is secondarily complain about it, try to get rid of it, prevent it.

262
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:18,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Affect the circumstances that have kind of indirectly propped it up.

263
00:28:24,025 --> 00:28:34,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you then still hold on to the attitude of not wanting it, of trying to get rid of it, craving for it to not be?

264
00:28:38,072 --> 00:28:40,733
[SPEAKER_01]: See, here now, the mind would expect some

265
00:28:42,998 --> 00:28:47,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Direct answer, they will undo this thing altogether, right away.

266
00:28:48,941 --> 00:28:51,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But the actual answer...

267
00:28:51,081 --> 00:29:04,767
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it can be a direct answer, but the... what that answer will tell you is that... it's not because you're making some sort of a mistake once, and then you provide an answer, and the mistake is removed, and you never ever go back to it.

268
00:29:05,668 --> 00:29:08,489
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of your other underlying attitude.

269
00:29:09,803 --> 00:29:18,307
[SPEAKER_01]: There is even more fundamental attitude to the attitude of, eh... craving for getting rid of the unpleasant feeling.

270
00:29:20,308 --> 00:29:23,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And that attitude is the attitude of ignorance.

271
00:29:23,689 --> 00:29:28,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of ignoring the nature of the feeling, pleasant or unpleasant or neutral.

272
00:29:30,973 --> 00:29:35,895
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why ignorance is the most fundamental existential error.

273
00:29:37,231 --> 00:29:40,232
[SPEAKER_01]: for which every single human being is responsible.

274
00:29:40,412 --> 00:29:42,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, every single being is responsible.

275
00:29:46,074 --> 00:29:58,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's through ignoring the nature of feeling, nature of, as we described it, nature of it to be arising on its own, manifested to you, for you, but you are not its creator.

276
00:29:58,319 --> 00:30:01,740
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in the way these feelings, where they come from.

277
00:30:02,521 --> 00:30:05,182
[SPEAKER_01]: You are just subjected to them in the same sense

278
00:30:06,300 --> 00:30:07,661
[SPEAKER_01]: The earth is subjected to weather.

279
00:30:09,102 --> 00:30:14,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You just can't even conceive preventing feelings from being there.

280
00:30:18,350 --> 00:30:33,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Not dwelling on that very nature long enough, that's how you maintain the fully gratuitous attitude of resisting feelings or indulging feelings.

281
00:30:34,414 --> 00:30:45,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any attitude towards feeling implies, as we said, the assumption that you can affect those feelings, either through prolonging them or getting rid of them.

282
00:30:47,172 --> 00:30:53,816
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize that whether you're prolonging... trying to prolong them or get rid of them, that is secondary to the presently enduring feeling that has arisen on its own.

283
00:30:54,857 --> 00:30:57,899
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop ignoring that, these attitudes will fade.

284
00:30:58,799 --> 00:31:01,921
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why ignorance cannot be undone accidentally.

285
00:31:02,522 --> 00:31:09,346
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot accidentally stumble upon the answer of the universe that will solve and prevent you from suffering ever again.

286
00:31:10,527 --> 00:31:12,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it wasn't... it's not a mistake.

287
00:31:12,947 --> 00:31:15,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an attitude that you maintain through your attitude.

288
00:31:16,168 --> 00:31:18,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So, avijjā is the cause for more avijjā.

289
00:31:18,208 --> 00:31:20,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring is the reason that there is ignoring.

290
00:31:21,389 --> 00:31:22,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of ignoring.

291
00:31:22,329 --> 00:31:22,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring what?

292
00:31:23,509 --> 00:31:33,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring the most fundamental, present, right in your face, nature of the five aggregates.

293
00:31:33,871 --> 00:31:36,432
[SPEAKER_01]: The most fundamental nature of the feeling.

294
00:31:39,980 --> 00:31:44,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Not dwelling on it long enough, or not dwelling on it at all.

295
00:31:47,203 --> 00:31:59,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You could see what a... how different this approach is, this approach with meditation is, than what you'll be commonly... commonly doing when you're practicing focusing or concentration through some technique.

296
00:32:01,689 --> 00:32:04,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you can also see which one of the two methods were more likely

297
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:10,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Result in your cessation active ignoring.

298
00:32:11,303 --> 00:32:15,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ceasing of your active ignoring of certain truths that are right in front of you.

299
00:32:16,426 --> 00:32:18,407
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can also see it cannot be given to you.

300
00:32:20,368 --> 00:32:26,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody cannot do that for you, make you stop ignoring the nature of the feeling that's present in your experience right here, right now.

301
00:32:28,452 --> 00:32:32,774
[SPEAKER_01]: The most another person can do is talk about it rightly.

302
00:32:32,814 --> 00:32:34,655
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then make you make the right effort.

303
00:32:35,056 --> 00:32:35,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Right effort

304
00:32:37,412 --> 00:32:53,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Being, recognizing the nature of the feeling, for example, the nature of unpleasant feeling, and see it as an independently arisen, presently enduring phenomena, that even if you resist it, that is secondary to it.

305
00:32:53,635 --> 00:32:54,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in it.

306
00:32:55,655 --> 00:32:56,155
[SPEAKER_01]: That's...

307
00:32:56,235 --> 00:33:04,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop resisting unpleasant feeling, for example, not by deciding to not resist the unpleasant feeling, but by fully

308
00:33:07,027 --> 00:33:11,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Overriding it with the understanding that whether you resist or not makes no difference.

309
00:33:12,271 --> 00:33:13,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Resisting is futile.

310
00:33:14,252 --> 00:33:22,297
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how resisting fades.

311
00:33:22,317 --> 00:33:30,783
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ceasing to ignore the obvious futility of your resistance to your emotionally

312
00:33:32,964 --> 00:33:36,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotional... present... present emotional state.

313
00:33:40,786 --> 00:33:42,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you stop resisting it.

314
00:33:43,227 --> 00:33:44,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the futility of it.

315
00:33:47,248 --> 00:33:52,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Like seeing it while the presently enduring unpleasant feeling is there.

316
00:33:54,050 --> 00:33:56,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just thinking about it abstractly.

317
00:33:58,512 --> 00:34:02,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do sort of fall into abstraction, you go back to concrete thinking.

318
00:34:02,871 --> 00:34:04,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is always a feeling right here, right now.

319
00:34:06,832 --> 00:34:09,372
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the previous talks we've done, like when you ask yourself.

320
00:34:09,552 --> 00:34:21,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Like now, you might have drifted away with all this information, but you ask yourself, okay, so what am I feeling right here, right now, overall, on the most fundamental level that I can be aware of?

321
00:34:21,115 --> 00:34:28,576
[SPEAKER_01]: At the most general background in your mind, what basis of feeling is there?

322
00:34:29,996 --> 00:34:31,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I okay or am I not okay?

323
00:34:31,637 --> 00:34:32,377
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, neither.

324
00:34:36,078 --> 00:34:39,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any of those answers is the right answer, if that's how you feel at the time.

325
00:34:41,521 --> 00:34:46,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that that basis is is something that is not your doing.

326
00:34:47,143 --> 00:34:50,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That basis is something that it's there whether you want it to be or not.

327
00:34:51,845 --> 00:34:52,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not ignoring.

328
00:34:53,606 --> 00:34:59,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to spend as much time as possible towards dwelling on the nature of that basis.

329
00:34:59,449 --> 00:35:01,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of it being independent of you.

330
00:35:01,930 --> 00:35:03,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Being indifferent to you.

331
00:35:04,181 --> 00:35:07,783
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you stop maintaining all these gratuitous attitudes towards it.

332
00:35:08,944 --> 00:35:18,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitudes that imply ownership, control, entitlement, you have a right for this to not be there, you have a right for this to stay longer, and so on.

333
00:35:19,751 --> 00:35:26,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those attitudes are simply rooted in you ignoring the nature of things in regard to which those attitudes are being exercised.

334
00:35:30,958 --> 00:35:31,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're trying.

335
00:35:31,458 --> 00:35:34,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, imagine you're trying to break through a door.

336
00:35:36,321 --> 00:35:39,343
[SPEAKER_01]: You will keep trying for as long as there is a hope that you can do it.

337
00:35:42,165 --> 00:35:51,791
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, suddenly, you hear the possibility that maybe, maybe, there's no need to be breaking through this door.

338
00:35:52,912 --> 00:35:54,693
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no need to get rid of it.

339
00:35:57,196 --> 00:35:59,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you understand that truly there is no need.

340
00:35:59,377 --> 00:35:59,958
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just that.

341
00:36:00,018 --> 00:36:02,679
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand that you can't, even if you want to.

342
00:36:03,480 --> 00:36:04,480
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no door, actually.

343
00:36:04,500 --> 00:36:13,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fully cemented, concreted, metal block, painted as a door, inviting you to try and break it.

344
00:36:14,626 --> 00:36:16,007
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically what the feelings are.

345
00:36:17,343 --> 00:36:29,511
[SPEAKER_01]: They present themselves as if they are for you, which is why the attitude of ignoring that they have arisen on its own for you, that's why that attitude is developed.

346
00:36:29,752 --> 00:36:31,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why ignorance is beginningless.

347
00:36:31,873 --> 00:36:38,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Things do deceive you in your sense of ownership, because that's nothing other than sense of ownership.

348
00:36:39,298 --> 00:36:43,881
[SPEAKER_01]: You trying to get rid of that which has arisen on its own means you assuming the ownership and the right.

349
00:36:46,190 --> 00:36:49,673
[SPEAKER_01]: to have it disappear, or stay longer.

350
00:36:50,053 --> 00:36:53,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you you act as if you are its master and controller.

351
00:36:53,116 --> 00:36:57,540
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inherently the conceit I am and sense of self.

352
00:36:58,520 --> 00:37:00,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things deceive you into that.

353
00:37:00,302 --> 00:37:05,266
[SPEAKER_01]: They present themselves as if... well, they present themselves there for you.

354
00:37:06,087 --> 00:37:07,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you assume that you are.

355
00:37:09,129 --> 00:37:12,952
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are truly, because I am what I am, who I am, because

356
00:37:13,967 --> 00:37:17,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see all these things that are mine, all these things that are for me.

357
00:37:17,791 --> 00:37:21,455
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say all these things, I mean even the phenomenon of your perception and your feeling.

358
00:37:24,779 --> 00:37:27,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feelings present themselves as if they are controllable.

359
00:37:28,583 --> 00:37:35,170
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you just slot into that trap of trying to control them.

360
00:37:37,426 --> 00:37:38,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you suffer.

361
00:37:39,107 --> 00:37:46,632
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha himself said, you don't suffer because there is displeasure present, or you don't suffer because misfortune happens to you, and so on.

362
00:37:47,373 --> 00:37:50,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You suffer because there is craving present in regard to the present feeling.

363
00:37:51,836 --> 00:37:57,460
[SPEAKER_01]: So, presence of craving means presence of suffering in regard to the feeling.

364
00:37:57,480 --> 00:37:59,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Not presence of feeling means presence of suffering.

365
00:38:00,402 --> 00:38:02,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving means absence of suffering.

366
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:08,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove your gratuitous attitude.

367
00:38:08,661 --> 00:38:25,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude, there was gratuitous right from the start, but because you were ignoring the basis in regard to which you are exercising that attitude, you were not clear, you were muddled about the nature of your feeling, that's why you maintain the gratuitously assumed attitude that these feelings are for you.

368
00:38:27,812 --> 00:38:39,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, you will be trying to get rid of the displeasure, and trying to indulge and prolong the pleasure, and be completely distracted, muddled, deluded, in regard to the neither pleasure nor displeasure.

369
00:38:41,723 --> 00:38:46,007
[SPEAKER_01]: But by not ignoring it, you stop providing the basis for these attitudes.

370
00:38:46,567 --> 00:38:50,951
[SPEAKER_01]: And eventually, they will have to fade, because they require maintenance.

371
00:38:50,991 --> 00:38:52,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintenance of ignorance.

372
00:38:58,015 --> 00:39:02,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you learn to think rightly as well, correctly.

373
00:39:02,297 --> 00:39:06,598
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha called it, the master of his thought, the person who does this.

374
00:39:06,798 --> 00:39:19,563
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't be thinking anything out of greed, aversion, anything out of indulging or trying to get rid of things, anything out of delusion or ignoring, because he has stopped ignoring the fundamental nature of his experience, the fundamental nature of the Five Aggregates.

375
00:39:20,543 --> 00:39:25,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Ceasing to ignore the nature of the Five Aggregates ceases to fuel

376
00:39:28,008 --> 00:39:28,969
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the fire of assumption.

377
00:39:31,850 --> 00:39:38,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that burns out, completely, gone, what's left there is just the aggregates that have been freed from any assumptions.

378
00:39:38,635 --> 00:39:40,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And these are exactly assumptions.

379
00:39:40,216 --> 00:39:49,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume, at face value, that the feeling can be changed.

380
00:39:51,423 --> 00:39:51,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And see,

381
00:39:52,926 --> 00:39:59,010
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the unpleasant feeling, then you make all these effort actions, chase the experiences that will provide you with the pleasant feeling.

382
00:39:59,871 --> 00:40:01,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a separate experience now.

383
00:40:02,073 --> 00:40:04,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't dealt with the originally unpleasant feeling.

384
00:40:06,456 --> 00:40:12,681
[SPEAKER_01]: It ceased on its own, and now you're experiencing some other pleasure on account of your desperate actions that have been chasing it.

385
00:40:14,222 --> 00:40:16,543
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that feeling of that pleasure has arisen on its own.

386
00:40:17,024 --> 00:40:18,365
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's not a guarantee.

387
00:40:19,167 --> 00:40:22,969
[SPEAKER_01]: That the actions towards the world will result in always the same type of feeling.

388
00:40:24,630 --> 00:40:29,452
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's enough to then reveal the nature of its independence of you.

389
00:40:30,913 --> 00:40:38,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, as I said, the Putujana doesn't know any other escape from the original unpleasant feeling, except sensuality.

390
00:40:41,038 --> 00:40:43,699
[SPEAKER_01]: As in replacing it with another experience of pleasant feeling.

391
00:40:45,107 --> 00:40:50,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Not dealing with the fundamental nature of being affected by displeasure.

392
00:40:53,449 --> 00:41:05,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not affected, if you cease to be affected by unpleasant feeling, if you cease to resist it in that ignorant manner, why would you be needing to replace it with any other feeling then?

393
00:41:06,553 --> 00:41:06,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't.

394
00:41:07,213 --> 00:41:12,775
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for somebody who is free from aversion, he becomes free from sensuality as well, hand in hand.

395
00:41:14,314 --> 00:41:16,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Free from sensuality is free from aversion.

396
00:41:19,016 --> 00:41:21,057
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much the state of anāgāmī.

397
00:41:29,061 --> 00:41:39,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ceasing to ignore the fundamental nature of the presently enduring feeling, whatever that feeling is, pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

398
00:41:41,067 --> 00:41:41,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

399
00:41:45,863 --> 00:41:51,125
[SPEAKER_01]: You can cease to ignore when you begin to learn what you have been ignoring.

400
00:41:54,307 --> 00:42:02,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Not ignoring the nature of feeling doesn't mean, like, you are super aware of, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling this every second of your experience.

401
00:42:02,811 --> 00:42:10,834
[SPEAKER_01]: It means the attitude of being muddled, unclear, unfamiliar with its nature has been abandoned.

402
00:42:10,974 --> 00:42:12,475
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you abandon the attitude of that?

403
00:42:13,335 --> 00:42:14,476
[SPEAKER_01]: By becoming familiar.

404
00:42:16,788 --> 00:42:18,649
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing clarity in regard to it.

405
00:42:18,769 --> 00:42:20,549
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you become familiar and develop clarity?

406
00:42:20,589 --> 00:42:22,410
[SPEAKER_01]: By making the effort to do so.

407
00:42:23,090 --> 00:42:24,391
[SPEAKER_01]: By bringing it to your mind.

408
00:42:25,451 --> 00:42:30,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't let yourself get... not let... not letting yourself get distracted and dwell on it.

409
00:42:30,053 --> 00:42:30,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwell on its nature.

410
00:42:33,754 --> 00:42:41,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, with, you know, guidance of suttas and dhamma talks and so on, gives you pointers where to look for, but you must be making that effort to look for it.

411
00:42:44,413 --> 00:42:47,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Because not looking for it means automatically ignoring it.

412
00:42:49,657 --> 00:42:53,480
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not about chasing particular feelings or particular issues that might bother you.

413
00:42:54,641 --> 00:43:06,449
[SPEAKER_01]: As we started this whole thing, it's basically stepping back, going... developing that peripheral vision that will... that will reveal the nature of any of those particular things.

414
00:43:06,850 --> 00:43:08,311
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we started this by saying,

415
00:43:09,391 --> 00:43:15,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you were angry or annoyed or whatever else, what was a common thread through all of those experiences?

416
00:43:15,976 --> 00:43:17,317
[SPEAKER_01]: It was that they're unpleasant.

417
00:43:18,098 --> 00:43:21,120
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the common thread to all the unpleasant experiences?

418
00:43:21,380 --> 00:43:22,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily just anger or something.

419
00:43:24,102 --> 00:43:26,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the common thread is that you resist it.

420
00:43:26,745 --> 00:43:33,610
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no unpleasant experience that doesn't have simultaneously present resistance in regard to it.

421
00:43:35,551 --> 00:43:37,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless, of course, you're already an arahant.

422
00:43:39,698 --> 00:43:49,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, any time, any particular thing touched you in an unpleasant way, emotionally, means there was resistance to that.

423
00:43:52,209 --> 00:43:53,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have two options here.

424
00:43:53,750 --> 00:44:07,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Try and control, manipulate, prevent the world, or simply remove the attitude of resistance in regard to anything that can touch you.

425
00:44:12,152 --> 00:44:15,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And only one of these attitudes will result in freedom from suffering.

